You must return a structured object (EstimationResult), not Markdown. The
presentation instructions above describe this application's local rendering only.

Required fields:
- summary (string, 10-1200 chars): a paragraph summarising the estimation. If the
  project is out of scope or the description is too vague to estimate with at
  least {{ low_confidence_threshold }}% confidence, the summary MUST start with
  "{{ out_of_scope_prefix }}" and explain why.
- confidence_pct (int, 0-100): how confident you are about the estimation given
  the description.
- phases (list, 1-8 items): each phase has name (1-64 chars), duration_weeks
  (1-52), cost_eur (0-1,000,000) and summary (10-600 chars).
- total_duration_weeks (int, 1-104): total calendar weeks across all phases.
- total_cost_eur (int, 0-2,000,000 whole euros): MUST equal the exact sum of
  every phase.cost_eur.

Hard constraints (will be re-validated; you will be re-prompted on violation):
- phases sum: total_cost_eur == sum(phase.cost_eur for every phase).
- low-confidence: if confidence_pct < {{ low_confidence_threshold }}, summary
  MUST start with "{{ out_of_scope_prefix }}".

When you declare "{{ out_of_scope_prefix }}" (confidence_pct < {{ low_confidence_threshold }}):
- Use total_cost_eur = 0, total_duration_weeks = 1.
- Return exactly ONE placeholder phase: name "Not estimated", duration_weeks 1,
  cost_eur 0, summary "Cannot be sized without more information."
- This keeps the response well-formed; downstream filters handle the user-facing message.

When you do estimate (confidence_pct >= {{ low_confidence_threshold }}): COMPUTE
the totals from the phases — do not pick a round number and then back-fit phases
to it. Decide every phase first, then set total_cost_eur to the exact arithmetic
sum of every phase.cost_eur, and total_duration_weeks to the sum of every
phase.duration_weeks (sequential delivery). Re-read your own answer before
returning and confirm both sums match.
